In article <YaAYAJC00XocQ21EQC@andrew.cmu.edu> aw1s+@andrew.cmu.edu (Andrew David Weiland) writes:
>I'd like to suggest the following changes for Patch Level 8 (hope it's
>not too late):

Unfortunately, it's very late. The decision was made some time ago that PL8 
would be for bug fixes and port enhancements only. These would have to go into
3.1.

>
>1.  Identify traps.
>	As is (PL 7) the only way to tell what a trap is after you escaped it
>is to go back and possibly activate it again.  This isn't a problem for
>pits or bear traps.  But sometimes i'll walk through a rust trap, arrow
>trap, dart trap, or squeaky board and miss the message.  I don't want to
>go through a rust trap twice.  The '/' command should identify traps (^)
>that the player has found in PL 8.

Strictly speaking, the '/' (whatis) command already does this, but *only* if
the '^' is the current symbol for that square. Thus, arrow and dart traps may 
be obscured. This is not really a problem, however, since if you a) know that 
there is indeed a trap on that square, b) have a reasonable suspicion that 
something shot out at you, and c) can use the whatis to find out *what* it 
shot at you, logical deduction should take care of the rest. 

>2.  Confirm a swim
>	I don't know if anyone else has this problem, but i've lost 2 1/2
>characters who accidentally walked into a water-filled area.  The
>water-filled area was visible on screen, but i either mis-keyed or
>temporarily lost presence of mind, because i walked into the area. (The
>1/2 was a character who had an amulet of life-saving, that was used up).
>
>	I can't think of a reason for entering a water-filled area, unless one
>is feeling suicidal.  I think Nethack should challenge the player's
>intellegence, not his keystroking.  In PL 8, the program should confirm
>"really walk into water[y/n]" whenever a character enters a visible
>water-filled area.

I empathize. I've lost 2 characters at the castle in this sort of way. #1 
fell into the same abyssal trapdoor *twice* without FR (the first time he had 
an LS amulet). #2 went skinny-dipping. I've also had very promising characters 
walk into known sleeping-gas traps.

But I don't campaign for a change: you can't expect NetHack to second-guess 
your intentions. There has to be line drawn somewhere, whether it be at giving
a dialog when you are about to attack peaceful or tame creatures (which is
modifiable by personal preference), or whether NetHack should decide for you
that 3 hill orcs are safe, but 4 is too risky, given your current 
hp/ac/weapon/bonuses, etc. etc..  (i.e. get NetHack to play your game for you).

There's nothing to stop an enterprising programming genius to write a 
NetHack "filter" that would make these decisions for you, of course. But that
would be an extension to, rather than "NetHack" itself. (My guess is it would
be extremely *difficult* to do, as well, since the game is so sophisticated.
I think the original Rogue-a-matic was at Ph.D. level, and the game is *much*
bigger now).

>3.  Repeat command
>
>	As is, the repeat command is okay.  It relieves tedium, and stops
>repeating commands when a character is hit.  But it doesn't stop when a
>character starts feeling hungry, or if the character is
>blind/hallucinating/confused when the blindness/hallucinations/confusion
>wears off.  When the character is repeat-searching, the repeats don't
>stop when a secret door or trap is found.
>	I believe the repeat command should halt whenever the character's
>condition changes (to hungry or weak, from confused or blind, etc), or
>when a trap/secret door is found, in addition to when a character is
>hit.  If i'm repeat-resting after drinking a
>blindness/hallucination/confusion potion, i'd like to stop whenever i
>can see again/stop hallucinating/don't feel confused.  When i find a
>secret door, i'm not going to keep searching.

The same sort of reply as above can be made: how is NetHack supposed to know 
that you are only searching for *one* concealed thing? There could be multiple 
secret doors/passageways and/or traps in your immediate vicinity, and you may 
want to find *all* of them. And certainly resting is an ambiguous state to the 
program, also. Some people rest when they are confused, some don't. If you are 
both stunned and hallucinating, which change-of-state should cause NetHack 
to terminate your repeated rest command?

>	Btw, i read earlier that scrolls have different effects while the
>reader is confused.  The worst of these is the genocide scroll, where
>the reader wipes out his own character class, and of course dies.  I
>tried this out (accidentally) and not only didn't die, but wiped out an
>entire race (in this case, liches).  Either the effects are random, or a
>blessed scroll works normally even if the reader is stupid enough to
>read it while confused.  In an earlier game, enchant weapon worked as
>described with confusion, not granting pluses but making the weapon
>rustproof.

I believe (I haven't checked the code on this) that, confusion or not, the
*only* scrolls that will wipe out a whole "class" (i.e. everything that has
a particular letter) are blessed ones. So that scroll that wiped out all 
liches *could* have been used for something more useful if you weren't  
confused. Maybe liches weren't a problem for your character (magic resistance, 
cold resistance, and lots of holy water perhaps?), but you were still
vulnerable to lots of dragons, so you would have rather trashed them.

Moral: Of the scrolls that do different things when you are confused, most 
give *less* control to the player, thus they are not as useful. Some, like 
enchant weapon, do things when confused that can't be done easily by any other 
means. So, when you are confused, KNOW WHAT YOU ARE DOING when you read a
scroll. Unidentified scrolls are always dangerous, and anything you have never 
read-while-confused before might generate unpredictable results.

(Actually, it's kind of funny to watch an super-character *try* to confuse
him or herself on purpose to achieve a desired scroll effect. Potions of 
confusion usually don't figure very high on inventory priorities, and rotten 
food or Umber Hulks are just *so* hard to come by when you need them...).

>Just my .02 zorkmids,

Appreciated. Just giving my personal counter-0.02 zorkmids also...

